# Interior comment inside a call argument's NESTED collection literal —
# live regression pin, and the NESTING cell of the canonical form.
#
# CELL (Core #12): axis = nesting depth. The comment sits two containers
# deep — inside the `[…]` array that is itself an argument of `Entry(…)`.
# It is the cell that makes the canonical form observable: *a fill-emitted
# container with an interior comment breaks fully, and so does every
# ancestor fill-emitted container on the path to it.* The argument tuple
# has no comment of its own and still explodes, because the array inside
# it does.
#
# ⚠ SCOPE OF THE PIN — the `Expr::Call` path, NOT `Expr::StructLiteral`.
# `gg fmt` is parse-only, so `Entry(1, […])` is an `Expr::Call` in fmt's
# view; the `Expr::StructLiteral` arm is unreachable here and carries no
# red. See `fmt_collection_literal_interior_struct_no_args.gg`.
#
# INDENT: the sentinel lands at 12 spaces, not 8 — statement body 4, the
# exploded argument tuple 8, the exploded inner array 12. An 8-space
# expectation would be the pre-canonical `Entry(1, [` shape, where the
# argument tuple stayed flat while its child broke; that shape is not
# reachable and not intended.
#
# RED-VERIFIED against the pre-fix binary: `# inner comment` escaped the
# nested array entirely and became a leading comment of `print(e.a)` —
#     Entry e = Entry(1, [10, 20])
#     # inner comment
#     print(e.a)
#
# The real-world instance is `compiler/data/resources.gg`, whose two long
# `BuiltinMethodDecl` comment blocks were ejected past the closing `])` to
# indent 4, where they read as documenting the NEXT table entry. They now
# sit at indent 12, immediately above the row they were written against.

struct Entry:
    int a
    Vector[int] xs

void main():
    Entry e = Entry(1, [
        10,
        # inner comment
        20,
    ])
    print(e.a)
